 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d033: inhibitory knot (k=9)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.3 and radius 1.88 with 9 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.34) and a 4-dim charge; 10 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 34 couplings (76% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.94 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.47 bonds per particle, largest molecule 9.0 particles, 7.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.120 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.116  random bonds 0.116\n  xor      0.604 vs ESN 0.517\u00b10.016  [beat]  no bonds 0.591  random bonds 0.502\n  narma10  0.335 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.303  random bonds 0.286\n  latch    0.919 vs ESN 0.684\u00b10.004  [beat]  no bonds 0.921  random bonds 0.924\n  mnist14  0.633 vs ESN 0.855\u00b10.018  [below]  no bonds 0.445  random bonds 0.615\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly matters on xor: the bonds its copies chose beat both no bonds and random bonds.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
